The docs indicate that this detection works fine for for-loops, for instance:

``` js
for (var j = 0; j < items.length; ++j) {
    doSomething(items[j]);
}
```

Where it detects that `j` is modified here.

Right. But it seems like the intent of the rule, based on the docs and even the rule name, was to detect unchanged _conditions_. Not to check that every variable in the condition is modified.

Especially given this example:

``` js
// OK, the result of this binary expression is changed in this loop.
while (node !== root) {
    doSomething(node);
    node = node.parent;
}
```

``` javascript
var x = 100, y, offset = 12;
for (y = 100; y > (x - offset); y--) {
    // ...
}
```

The above example produces the following errors:

```
error  'x' is not modified in this loop       no-unmodified-loop-condition
error  'offset' is not modified in this loop  no-unmodified-loop-condition
```

`y` is changing in this loop, so `x` and `offset` should be allowed to be constants, correct?
